{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck","id":1020225,"title":"to what extend did the \"dark ages\" really suck?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-21T01:37:03-08:00","id":1020225,"title":"to what extend did the \"dark ages\" really suck?","content":"for common man i mean.<br /> here's some points to consider. since reality is consensual this means that this was an era when bloodletting did indeed work. the system of 5 humours was true. touched by a king could cure leprosy. prayers to saints/angels could - on occasion really cure diseases.<br /> and of course your local mage with the appropriate pillars could cast a healing/curing spell on everyone he met without fear of exploding. or being sniped by man in black<br /> <br /> likewise not all bygones were harmful to humanity etc","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T04:14:06-08:00","id":1020267,"title":"","content":"It is a perspective thing.<br /> Ostracism was common.<br /> There where many localized Realities, as in every village has its own rules. You had little to say in most cases.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>So, this Zen Master walks up to a hot dog stand and says: &quot;Make me one with everything!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12022-nonsense","name":"Nonsense","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12022_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"815"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T05:00:03-08:00","id":1020289,"title":"","content":"Food was difficult to get anywhere, meaning a community lived or died on the quality of the yearly harvest. The reason we have hunger nowadays isn't because of scarcity - our crop production is so great that Americans can waste 40% of it and not starve - it's because no one wants to front the cost of taking food from where it's abundant to where it's lacking. In the Dark Ages, there simply was not any way to transport anything but heavily salted foods over long distances. No way that was available to common people.<br /> <br /> And that's the rub, isn't it? The Dark Ages sucked because so many conveniences we take for granted now were not Consensual back in the day. At least, not in a Consensus that allowed them to be used by ordinary folk. Say what you want about the Technocratic Union, but their system did put technological power in peoples' hands. That power has since vastly improved humanity's lot in life.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T05:34:43-08:00","id":1020314,"title":"","content":"The boon of Dark Ages for Mages was lack of Consensus. The worst of Dark Ages for mortals was lack of Consensus. The common man had mostly as hard a time as stated in the Renascence-era books about the Dark Ages. Scarce food, scarce knowledge, scarce everything. It was an event on itself to reach puberty, and a feat to live to the forties.<br /> <br /> And them you throw the supernatural in the pot. Of course, there were benevolent creatures back then, as there are nowadays. With luck your village could be home to a Mage saint who delivered healing miracles far more than the wrath of an unforgiving God. Maybe you're defended from the horrors of the night by a Salubri Warrior with heart of gold. Your people could have the friendship of powerful Changers or the Good Folk. It was a time of heroes, more overtly than our modern times.<br /> <br /> But it mostly happened because the supernatural was unchecked. Bygones of every kind still could exist in this earthly realm, and they were dangerous more often than not. Most supernaturals aren't very caring, as most mortals aren't, either. Not even human law had a fraction of the efficacy it have today (with all the problems of today), just imagine what it was for supernaturals. They roamed the land freely, and even their indifference would harm feeble mortals. And some could be astoundingly malicious.<br /> <br /> Mostly in the Dark Ages the common man was alone. Whatever came their way, they wouldn't have much more than themselves to brace it. Even the nearest neighbor could be hours away, lords and armies would could be days, even at horse.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T06:36:14-08:00","id":1020350,"title":"","content":"Other things that the Dark Ages didn't have:<ul><li>Guns. It is said &quot;God created man and Sam Colt made them equal&quot;. Before the advent of efficient firearms, the common peasant had only his farming or crafting tools, and whatever strength and skill from said farming or crafting to defend himself with. Any bandit group with a modicum of training (like, for instance, the now-jobless veterans of a short-lived war) could run roughshod over the countryside. The only thing the people could do is hope the local Lord could (or would) send knights or mercenaries to take care of the matter. While that system functioned, it meant that the third estate was locked into a compact with their masters just to get by, and one that massively restricted their autonomy.</li> <li>Speaking of, Social Mobility. Classes were rigid structures in those days. While today the idea of the self-made man is a cliche, back in the Dark Ages it was just about impossible. Partly because of the above servitude to a Lord, but also because of the twin barriers of societal norms and lack of education. It simply wasn't done to try rising above the station God, in His Providence, assigned to you from birth. Even the ideas of defying the social order wouldn't hardly occur to a peasant, who was cut off from any sort of intellectual discourse. It wouldn't be until years later, during what Mage calls the Sorcerer's Crusade, that moveable type would come into existence and jumpstart mass media. No way a wheat farmer in the middle of nowhere could afford even a single book, which limits his options even on a conceptual level.</li> <li>Freedom of Thought. Going off the above yet again, the Dark Ages didn't tolerate a difference of opinion, especially not concerning politics or religion. What we would call Alternate Religious Denominations today went by a different name back then: Heresies. While a popular divergence from Vatican dogma could blossom in a remote area and obtain a reasonable following, they eventually invited the wrath of the Church and the authorities that pledged loyalty to it. Imagine, then, being a Muslim, Jew, or follower of the Old Gods during this time. Nowadays, religious tensions simmer under the surface, flaring up into relatively minor violence that is condemned (though not always stopped) by the powers that be and the media. Modern man has the knowledge that they have a right to believe as they chose, and the government couldn't intervene. In the Dark Ages, it's the government that is likely to drag you to the pyre themselves, just for the crime of <a href=\"https://youtu.be/E1ZZeCDGHJE?list=PLhyKYa0YJ_5A2WfrnfPglTL_ZCPDN_rDT\" target=\"_blank\">thinking The Father is of a higher station that The Son in the Holy Trinity</a>. (Yes, wrong time period, but it illustrates my point.)</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T07:13:10-08:00","id":1020379,"title":"","content":"Adding further.<br /> <br /> The gun thing is an overlooked one. Industrial Revolution not only made guns functional in the way we're used to think, made them cheap and easy, and that transformed how military works. Back in the medieval being a peasant not only means you're weak, also means you're barred from military service unless a great need makes room in it for you. Equipment is costly, and if the local lord isn't in a great need for more recruits, you have to afford your own stuff if you want to give it a try. Of course, need created exceptions, but it wasn't default.<br /> <br /> Also, if the lord or king could send knights to take care of bandits, it isn't at all like modern police. First it works slowly, the knights will hunt known bandits and patrol a place for a time, but you can't call them to your home if you're invaded. You're still vulnerable and on your own until the bandits are found or go away. Only truly overt displays of force are better protected against, like raids and sacks. Second, well... you are vulnerable to the knights themselves. They are usually better than the bandits, but they're still a possible threat with the added authority's abuse.<br /> <br /> You have a few options if you're a peasant, but not great ones. First, you can be a villain, a villager who got permission to live in the land without working on it nor serving the nobles. Villains are some of the people most used to actually deal in money, but they have to be in it for some very nasty or unusual jobs sometimes. They are the most common mercenaries, and also most used as helpers to the officers that need something done among the peasants. You can also be a bourgeoisie, for now it simply means you're a trader by profession, or an artisan, that is almost the same socially speaking. Both deal a lot with &quot;urban&quot; areas and money proper. With a very good amount of ability and luck you can even become rich. That won't bring you up in the totem-pole, though. A rich peasant is still a peasant, and do not have rights to the juicier boom of a plot of land. At most you can pay for some personal guards among unemployed mercenaries, maybe dream with paying for the equipment for your son to become a squire, so he can aim for becoming a knight. A lowly one, still.<br /> <br /> On the religious side, you have two options to do things for the vast population that aren't truly Christians. You can run right into the fire with them if discovered, or you can stick to IRL laws on the matter. That's a little trickier. The Church only claimed domain over baptized Christians, so unconverted Pagans, Muslins and Jews are outside its reach. But the Christian Kings almost always declare that any non-Christian inhabitant is exiled from the realm. It kind of forces you into conversion, and into the claws of the Church.<br /> <br /> Remember also that it is trickier to obey the Church when no one have a bible to read, or knows how to read, and nothing keeps local authorities from taking some liberties with the scripture. The Church is a complex thing in those times, because perverting the bible is as practiced as burning people for perverting it, and mostly both are done by the same people. Church politics do more for defining who will burn than actual attendance to the bible.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T08:01:16-08:00","id":1020401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1020350#post1020350\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Other things that the Dark Ages didn't have:[*]Speaking of, Social Mobility. Classes were rigid structures in those days. While today the idea of the self-made man is a cliche, back in the Dark Ages it was just about impossible. Partly because of the above servitude to a Lord, but also because of the twin barriers of societal norms and lack of education. It simply wasn't done to try rising above the station God, in His Providence, assigned to you from birth. Even the ideas of defying the social order wouldn't hardly occur to a peasant, who was cut off from any sort of intellectual discourse. It wouldn't be until years later, during what Mage calls the Sorcerer's Crusade, that moveable type would come into existence and jumpstart mass media. No way a wheat farmer in the middle of nowhere could afford even a single book, which limits his options even on a conceptual level.</div> </div> </div> </div>That one actually varies quite a lot by when and where you were in that time period. It was actually a time of a great deal of social upheaval, which meant there were a lot of opportunities for carving out a new position. For instance, in many parts of Europe, if you could unseat a knight in a formal competition you got his armor, weapons, and horse. For all intents and purposes, you became a minor noble*. A lot of the customs you refer to actually came later in the Medieval Period.<br /> <br /> *You had to have a horse first, but in the early days of the armored knight they weren't as large or as specialized, so it wasn't quite the hurdle it would be later.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 12-21-2016, 11:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T09:29:47-08:00","id":1020439,"title":"","content":"There's a couple major problems with this sort of question, really.<br /> <br /> Most of the everybody-knows facts about the &quot;Dark Ages&quot; are basically complete nonsense.<br /> Most of the genre-typical fiction tropes about things set in the &quot;Dark Ages&quot; are basically complete nonsense.<br /> <br /> But the Dark Ages <i>line </i>for WoD is specifically a very gothic, deeply religious and baroque take on the era, so some of that nonsense is true? The Church of DA WoD is nothing like the religious culture of the real-world antique and medieval period, for instance, because it exists as a vehicle for pathos-ridden tales about vampires in an Anne Rice style.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck#post1020225\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">for common man i mean.<br /> here's some points to consider. since reality is consensual this means that this was an era when bloodletting did indeed work. the system of 5 humours was true. touched by a king could cure leprosy. prayers to saints/angels could - on occasion really cure diseases.<br /> and of course your local mage with the appropriate pillars could cast a healing/curing spell on everyone he met without fear of exploding. or being sniped by man in black<br /> <br /> likewise not all bygones were harmful to humanity etc</div> </div> </div> </div>The DA:M and Revised line of thought on this period is not that the antique/medieval periods were the Dung Ages of popular imagining, but rather that the biggest (magely) problem of the era is <i>neglect; </i>the Fellowships coalesce in this period because mages are losing sight of their duties to the rest of humanity and focusing on intertribal warfare and the acquisition of mystical power. Even compassionate magical societies tended to turn a blind eye to problems on the ground <i>today </i>in favor of achieving a long-distant objective; the Batini cause is noble, but the Batini of the DA era are content to manipulate (and, if need be, assassinate) Sleepers in order to eventually arrive at their desired objective - they don't see educating Sleepers and engaging with them as equals and peers as a useful application of their time.<br /> <br /> The Wyck and the Aeduna taught mankind medicine, agriculture, weaving, metallurgy, how to domesticate animals...<br /> <br /> ...the witches of the Old Faith think that magic is something that's inherited in the blood, and while they might heal your cow with herbcraft, they won't teach you how it's done, and they spend most of their time pursuing feuds with rival witches and other magical societies.<br /> <br /> This is why the Craftmasons and Order are the much-needed boot to the ass that turns Fellowships into Traditions: Mage society had lost sight of its <i>purpose </i>amidst the feuding, and grown arrogant from the power of a Mythic Age. The Masons have a religious disdain for anything that isn't born of humble roots; while I don't think they're as noble as some folks do, that sort of focus was completely absent from the Fellowships.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T13:25:27-08:00","id":1020602,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> and <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1653_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1653\" data-vbnamecard=\"1653\">Five Eyes</a>, the main point here is to describe the Dark Ages of WoD. It isn't the actual Middle Ages, but a look to the past rooted in modern popular tropes, with added gothic themes and food for drama. As the WoD is a dark depiction of our world, the Dark Ages are a dark depiction of our past. It isn't our past <i>per se</i>.<br /> <br /> Just to make it clear for everyone: IRL, most of our ideas about the so called Middle Ages came from a perspective established during the Renaissance. The intellectuals of this period praised pr\u00e9-Christian civilizations (not necessarily their religion, mind you, but their culture), and developed the <u>false</u> idea that the rough one thousand years of Roman Catholic Church &quot;domain&quot; on society resulted only on lost of knowledge and stagnation. Hence the term &quot;Dark Ages&quot;. This view was strongly accepted and endorsed by several intellectual circles since, but without actual analysis of remnants of the time. Recently the Neo-Paganism and similar movements from the last two centuries were among the louder proponents of this ideas, along with Atheist movements, for the obvious reason of those movements being at odds with the Church. This added more weight to the matter.<br /> <br /> That said, the last decades saw a greater development in historical research of the period, and revealed that most of previous assumptions were just prejudice, moved by disagreements with the Church and Christianity in general. The actual Middle Ages is a far more complex period of History, very nuanced through time and space. Most of things ascribed to it are rooted on half-truths and over-simplification. The Church isn't as good as it wanted to depict itself, of course, but as said, things were far more complex.<br /> <br /> We are talking here about World of Darkness' Dark Ages, though. It isn't a depiction of actual Middle Ages, but a dark setting for fantasy games. It can greatly benefit from new data on IRL Middle Ages that add to dramatic possibilities and general mood of the setting. But to ascribe it with more than its share of reality is to hurt both the setting and the fascinating period of our History.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T20:21:49-08:00","id":1020768,"title":"","content":"Actually, I think that it adds to the setting to accurately portray the High Middle Ages. There was a massive industrial renaissance in the High Middle Ages due to the invention of watermills and windmills, allowing work to be done without depending directly on the energy produced by man or animal. The High Middle Ages also benefited from massive increases in food production and population due to the Medieval Warm Period. The combination of increased industry and increased population increased the economic output of Europe by fourfold in two centuries, which meant that Europe during the High Middle Ages was more prosperous than at any time until the 1800s. Of course, that ended with the collapses that occurred during the Little Ice Age.<br /> <br /> The High Middle Ages would have been a Golden Age for the Kindred and a Dark Age for Mages. With growing human populations and growing economic productivity, more Kindred can be supported and more wealth can accumulate among the Kindred while the standardization of thought and education would have caused the Scourge to intensify. Mages would have been horrified by the metaphysical changes that started to occur as ideas of nation and trade replaced the ideas of kingdom and tribe and as the ideas of education and industry replaced the ideas of magic and religion.<br /> <br /> The Little Ice Age would have been a boon to Mages because it destroyed the faith that people had in the new ideas of nation, trade, education, and industry and forced them to embrace the old ideas of kingdom, tribe, magic, and religion. In the 1200s, people across Western Europe doubted the existence of Witches and the Devil because of everything good that was occurring across Europe, despite the influence of the Catholic Church. By the 1400s, people across Western Europe accepted the existence of Witches and the Devil because of everything bad that was occurring across Europe, despite the fragmenting of the Catholic Church. The changes that occurred between 1200 and 1400 birthed a new age of magic for Europe, as people stopped doubting the existence of supernatural forces.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T06:03:59-08:00","id":1020845,"title":"","content":"Not a great new insight but something to highlight what others have said, and an interesting but of trivia. In the middle ages 90% of the population was involved in agriculture in order to produce and distribute enough food for the populace. Today roughly 10% of the population is invovled with agricultural and we actually produce enough food to feed the world 3 times over.<br /> <br /> Yet children still die of starvation. <br /> <br /> Sorry for the sad note at the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/987-tuch","name":"Tuch","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar987_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"419"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T09:55:12-08:00","id":1020931,"title":"","content":"Don't be sorry for it, it is jut reality, and given the dark tone of the game, deserving of being noted.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11683\" data-vbnamecard=\"11683\">Aya Tari</a>, I'm not saying that the Middle Ages don't make for an interesting setting, nor that bits of it can help to improve the game.<br /> <br /> I'm saying that they are distinct things whatsoever. And, sadly, most of the setting was thought of with only the twisted renaissance view on the Dark Ages on mind. To make space for the real thing in the setting a lot of retcons would be needed. As in how the Scourge only gets worse in the 11th century and the Low Middle Ages is the time of the Order of Reason. It is counter-intuitive for the real thing, but also very well established in the setting.<br /> <br /> But as I said, in the end it is &quot;take the real deal with a grain of salt in the setting&quot;, not &quot;stay away of the real deal&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T12:45:46-08:00","id":1020976,"title":"","content":"i'm sorry but i do not get this &quot; guns are good&quot; things. take the scenario with these bandits. if the peasants had guns to fight them then so would these ex-mercenaries.<br /> and of course the mercs guns would be better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T12:47:13-08:00","id":1020977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1020768#post1020768\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, I think that it adds to the setting to accurately portray the High Middle Ages. There was a massive industrial renaissance in the High Middle Ages due to the invention of watermills and windmills, allowing work to be done without depending directly on the energy produced by man or animal. The High Middle Ages also benefited from massive increases in food production and population due to the Medieval Warm Period. The combination of increased industry and increased population increased the economic output of Europe by fourfold in two centuries, which meant that Europe during the High Middle Ages was more prosperous than at any time until the 1800s. Of course, that ended with the collapses that occurred during the Little Ice Age.<br /> <br /> The High Middle Ages would have been a Golden Age for the Kindred and a Dark Age for Mages. With growing human populations and growing economic productivity, more Kindred can be supported and more wealth can accumulate among the Kindred while the standardization of thought and education would have caused the Scourge to intensify. Mages would have been horrified by the metaphysical changes that started to occur as ideas of nation and trade replaced the ideas of kingdom and tribe and as the ideas of education and industry replaced the ideas of magic and religion.<br /> <br /> The Little Ice Age would have been a boon to Mages because it destroyed the faith that people had in the new ideas of nation, trade, education, and industry and forced them to embrace the old ideas of kingdom, tribe, magic, and religion. In the 1200s, people across Western Europe doubted the existence of Witches and the Devil because of everything good that was occurring across Europe, despite the influence of the Catholic Church. By the 1400s, people across Western Europe accepted the existence of Witches and the Devil because of everything bad that was occurring across Europe, despite the fragmenting of the Catholic Church. The changes that occurred between 1200 and 1400 birthed a new age of magic for Europe, as people stopped doubting the existence of supernatural forces.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> mages(depending on the group of course) were hardly against education and cities. just ask the batini/hermetics/choristers and individuals from other fellowships","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T13:00:52-08:00","id":1020982,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1020976#post1020976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i'm sorry but i do not get this &quot; guns are good&quot; things. take the scenario with these bandits. if the peasants had guns to fight them then so would these ex-mercenaries.<br /> and of course the mercs guns would be better.</div> </div> </div> </div>Guns are equalizing elements. It takes real strength and skill to kill with farming tools, or even real melee weapons. A gun has a dirt simple point and kill interface, which anyone can use so long as they know which direction the blam blams come out of.<br /> <br /> Moreover, anyone with experience in firearms combat knows that, when the bullets start flying, any shot can get lucky and drop you like a chump. This is why gun owners advertise their gun ownership with signs on their property. Unless the aggressors are stupid or desperate, burglars and the like think twice about taking on armed homes. It's simply not worth the risk, when weaker targets can be found elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Or, to put it simply, a melee battle comes down to who is stronger, more skilled, and better armed. A gun battle could go either way, and criminals are a superstitious and cowardly lot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T14:44:32-08:00","id":1021018,"title":"","content":"Yeah, none of this gun stuff jibes with real history, where firearms shaped the evolution of the professional army. Prior to that military service was the prerogative of the landholding military, supported by levies and mercenaries. What it did change was the aristocratic fortress household, which was the focus of medieval military action. Even the American Revolution was a product of a nucleus of professional soldiers assisted by the logistical nightmare of moving British forces around.<br /> <br /> Anyway, for much of the Middle Ages:<br /> * You are never a face in the crowd. Few communities are large enough to provide that anonymity.<br /> * You require permission to travel and enter communities, though there is such a thing as religious tourism. The fantasy inn doesn't really exist.<br /> * You are probably physically stronger and have better teeth than your modern counterpart. You are not that much shorter -- that happens later -- and you live a good while provided you survive childhood.<br /> * You do not have a private life. Being alone is seen as a hardship, practiced by monks. Even when you're wealthy you sleep in the same room as your staff.<br /> * You reach puberty in your mid-teens, not earlier as we do now.<br /> * In many places, if you're a man and possess a certain amount of money you are required to own basic arms and armour in case you're called up. You practice military skills regularly, such as after church.<br /> * Church is the centre of your social life and where important community decisions get made. You might even write on a church wall to leave a message.<br /> * You probably can't read or write unless you're clergy.<br /> * You might go hungry in a bad year, though people help each other out. Hunting without permission is poaching, and illegal. Meat is uncommon.<br /> * You get married young if you're rich, but don't consummate for a while. You marry in your 20s if you're a typical sort of person.<br /> * In battle, your goal is to take or hold a castle. If you're a knight or noble, you prefer to take other knights hostage. If you're not, winning your side of the siege is more important than killing anyone, but of anyone is visibly sadistic or breaks their word, all bets are off.<br /> * If you're Jewish, you're subject to murderous persecution at times and may belong to a special set of professions the Church discourages Christians from participating in, or which are simply convenient for non-Christians to engage in, such as medicine, certain types of trade and moneylending.<br /> * Instead of fancy jousting tournaments, knights (mostly hereditary martial arts experts trained from an early age) practice the bohurt/behourd, where they fight in a constrained area (like &quot;between two towns&quot;) and try not to kill each other. Winners get the losers' arms and armour. The Church disapproves of these.<br /> * If you wear any armour, it will be a gambeson, a padded jacket. You're wear a helmet too. If you're wealthy, your armour will be mail, supported by an increasing number of plates until the 14th century, when steel plating starts to become the primary form of protection -- still supported by mail. All &quot;mail&quot; is chainmail -- nothing else has that name, and ringmail doesn't exist. <br /> * People are not *that* superstitious. They believe in the evidence of their senses. The Church may ascribe symbolism to natural phenomena. You may believe in animals such as the Pard, which don't exist. You fear wolves, which will kill you if they're very hungry and you look like easy prey. People skilled in folk medicine combine ritual with pragmatic things, and don't always know where one ends and the other begins. People don't believe these folks have anything to do with the Devil yet. If Christian, you probably believe saints and Christian miracles play an active part in the world. You might believe in hidden spirits. You believe there was a literal Simon Magus so in that case, you believe in &quot;magic.&quot; If you're educated you might believe that certain rituals with Christian and Platonic elements influence the world, but if you're not educated you don't know anything about that.<br /> <br /> In the World of Darkness, you might believe other things. You may know that the Order of Hermes has autonomous fortresses like monasteries, but you're supposed to stay away from them. You now that supernatural forces exist in the woods, and that some of the wise people you know might really be pagans of some kind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:42:14-08:00","id":1021047,"title":"","content":"In addition to the above:<br /> <br /> *You receive nearly six months off from work during the year because of Sundays, Saint's Day, Market Days, etc.<br /> <br /> *A work day is considered to be six hours because people are expected to participate in family life. If you work twelve hours straight, you are considered to have worked two days straight, and the local clergy will likely castigate any boss who regularly works their people that hard outside of planting and harvest. When combined with the previous point, the average adult person in the High Middle Ages worked around 1200 hours a year, when the extended days for planting and harvest are included.<br /> <br /> *Slaves are rare outside of Eastern Europe and slave ownership of Christians is forbidden by the Church.<br /> <br /> *Serfs were paid in beer and meat during planting and harvest by their lord and the Church. Serfs also had right to land and, as long as they participated in planting and harvest, would not lose their land. Outside of famine, they rarely went hungry, as hungry serfs were unproductive serfs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T19:18:14-08:00","id":1021110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1020976#post1020976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">i'm sorry but i do not get this &quot; guns are good&quot; things. take the scenario with these bandits. if the peasants had guns to fight them then so would these ex-mercenaries.<br /> and of course the mercs guns would be better.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most of the time, there are no such a thing as a better gun. If you take a shot, you die. That said, first its as Bluecho said, guns are an equalizer. Melee weapons don't actually take as much strength as most people think, though the game does make such assumption, but still take a great deal of ability and training. Like, it takes you months to learn how to handle a sword simply for you not to hurt yourself in a battle. To effectively use it in real combat it takes even longer. With a couple hours of training, a normal person won't be an excellent shooter, but at least will be able to use it in an emergency, and have a reasonable chance of being effective.<br /> <br /> Second, given that we're talking about Middle Ages, there is the problem of cost. Those peasants aren't likely to have many actual weapons, and it isn't the same to improvise with tools, there are worlds of difference.<br /> <br /> But my personal focus is yet another, I don't think that an equalizer does all that difference (though anything is valid when you're all by yourself). Industrial Age Guns popularized military service. And military service is maybe the only universal path for social ascension. It was even more at the Middle Ages. Rulers pay well to have strong arms at their side. Domains are granted on the basis of military strength. The first step to actually ascend the medieval totem pole is to become a Knight. But that takes training and equipment.<br /> <br /> At times of need, the rulers will afford that, so at least some people will have the opportunity. Sometimes luck is upon you and you'll get yourself some used equip, and someone will train you. It happens. But the bulk of the army on most of the time will be comprised already of lesser nobles. Military service is the path for ascension, but is also quite literally cutthroat. You'll be risking you life and eventually the need will vanish for a time, and then someone will get the short straw. Like those bandits, a lot of them unemployed soldiers, but still far better trained and equipped than peasants.<br /> <br /> In such a context, nobles still want for their children to keep their places. We are too used to the renaissance idea of a palatial noble, the medieval noble is a man of war. They are trained and equipped from an early age (as everyone is trained and equipped at their future jobs). So if you got lucky to find some old weapons and armor and train for a couple months, you'll still have to stand up among professional Knights trained since children that afforded their own equipment since the beginning (as a given, IRL it would probably be still used, and a lot were not that good at fight, but as I said, WoD's Dark Ages).<br /> <br /> Now at least poor people can have as an option for employment military service. Its still risky, but its place in society changed a lot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1020977#post1020977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">mages(depending on the group of course) were hardly against education and cities. just ask the batini/hermetics/choristers and individuals from other fellowships</div> </div> </div> </div>The point of Aya wasn't education being present, but standardized. Mages want education, most of them. But each wants education on their own terms. Standardization worked mostly in favor of the Order of Reason, and now the Technocratic Union. Its also of note that education takes time and effort, and the technology promoted by the Order made Effects accessible to uneducated people once incorporated into the Consensus. In a Hermetic-Paradigm Consensus no one would be able to use a Crystal Ball for communication before a 6 to 12 months course, and it would still be damn hard to pass, because they think things have to be this hard. Power must come from knowledge, and knowledge must be conquered through blood, sweat and tears.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021018#post1021018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, none of this gun stuff jibes with real history, where firearms shaped the evolution of the professional army. Prior to that military service was the prerogative of the landholding military, supported by levies and mercenaries. What it did change was the aristocratic fortress household, which was the focus of medieval military action. Even the American Revolution was a product of a nucleus of professional soldiers assisted by the logistical nightmare of moving British forces around.<br /> <br /> Anyway, for much of the Middle Ages:<br /> * You are never a face in the crowd. Few communities are large enough to provide that anonymity. <b>Few if any, even great cities of the past got smaller. Just don't forget that people do go missing, specially in WoD</b><br /> * You require permission to travel and enter communities, though there is such a thing as religious tourism. The fantasy inn doesn't really exist. <b>Its worth of note that most supernaturals have plenty ways to deal with that, so they travel far more than common people.</b><br /> * You are probably physically stronger and have better teeth than your modern counterpart. You are not that much shorter -- that happens later -- and you live a good while provided you survive childhood.<b> It can works both ways in the WoD, depending on what will be better for the chronicle. Short-lived people makes it easier to be forgotten in a community, specially for unaging monsters that take a little care. I don't think the rest derails to much from setting, but if someone wants short, weak, toothless people...</b><br /> * You do not have a private life. Being alone is seen as a hardship, practiced by monks. Even when you're wealthy you sleep in the same room as your staff.<b> Pretty much. Being a loner is the easiest way to stand out as suspicious, that's why so many tales link loners to human-like monsters or monsters' victims.</b><br /> * You reach puberty in your mid-teens, not earlier as we do now.<b> But you can be engaged sexually earlier, specially in the WoD, and few people will raise a hand against it.</b><br /> * In many places, if you're a man and possess a certain amount of money you are required to own basic arms and armour in case you're called up. You practice military skills regularly, such as after church.<b> Take care not to show any inhuman strength, you won't train alone.</b><br /> * Church is the centre of your social life and where important community decisions get made. You might even write on a church wall to leave a message.<b> If you can't be at church for any reason, people will really get suspicious. And you'll get lonely. A Ghoul that can't enter church is in it for a far, far harder time then than nowadays.</b><br /> * You probably can't read or write unless you're clergy.<b> But you still can learn to get a few things. Not enough to read a book, as if you would ever get your hands on one, but enough for write and read some symbols in the wall of the church. Sometimes nobles are exceptions, and learn to write and read. Sometimes.</b><br /> * You might go hungry in a bad year, though people help each other out. Hunting without permission is poaching, and illegal. Meat is uncommon.<b> Outside a bad year, though, your diet is rich. Royalty and, to a lesser degree, nobility get so reliable on meat, the wild animals being legally the King's property and hunting rights given to nobles, that the varied diet of the peasant is far better to health, and fresher, too. Or you can go all WoD style and ignore it altogether, I would probably do that and make peasants eat dirt.</b><br /> * You get married young if you're rich, but don't consummate for a while. You marry in your 20s if you're a typical sort of person.<b> Also, marriage isn't that formal for most people. Only if your marriage was arranged or is incredibly good from a social/economic standpoint you'll get a big party. Marrying in a big fashion because of love is a completely alien thought.</b><br /> * In battle, your goal is to take or hold a castle. If you're a knight or noble, you prefer to take other knights hostage. If you're not, winning your side of the siege is more important than killing anyone, but of anyone is visibly sadistic or breaks their word, all bets are off.<b> Yes, IRL war was an ugly affair, but far less ugly for the soldier themselves than most people think. They don't grow on trees, y'know? But for the WoD, you can go for a more grisly thing, at your discretion.</b><br /> * If you're Jewish, you're subject to murderous persecution at times and may belong to a special set of professions the Church discourages Christians from participating in, or which are simply convenient for non-Christians to engage in, such as medicine, certain types of trade and moneylending.<b> Sometimes you are exiled for not being a Christian (at least you're not pagan), but that won't be such a thing until the Reform. Then you're in problems, and can be required to conversion if you want to keep your house. Obviously, in the WoD the church is on the hunt for you a little earlier, but not that earlier, either.</b><br /> * Instead of fancy jousting tournaments, knights (mostly hereditary martial arts experts trained from an early age) practice the bohurt/behourd, where they fight in a constrained area (like &quot;between two towns&quot;) and try not to kill each other. Winners get the losers' arms and armour. The Church disapproves of these.<b> Other games and rules appear from time to time, people are trying to take a hand at how to have this kind of fun with more safety. Eventually, but only later on, jousting will be more popular because it will devise better equipment to balance fun and safety. Joust is also full of rules from the start, indeed most of the rules are the same in the last nine hundred years. In WoD I wouldn't recommend to change that a lot, but the dark corners of the community can hold bloodier tournaments.</b><br /> * If you wear any armour, it will be a gambeson, a padded jacket. You're wear a helmet too. If you're wealthy, your armour will be mail, supported by an increasing number of plates until the 14th century, when steel plating starts to become the primary form of protection -- still supported by mail. All &quot;mail&quot; is chainmail -- nothing else has that name, and ringmail doesn't exist.<b> The &quot;full plate&quot; popular in fantasy only really appears on the bent from 16th to 17th century, with earlier versions starting at the very end of the 15th. They appear already going out, given recent developments favoring light protection or no protection at all. You also have Joust's Armor keeping the image of the full plate, but it actually never served any purpose on true combat, it was only of any use, well, on joust.</b><br /> * People are not *that* superstitious. They believe in the evidence of their senses. The Church may ascribe symbolism to natural phenomena. You may believe in animals such as the Pard, which don't exist. You fear wolves, which will kill you if they're very hungry and you look like easy prey. People skilled in folk medicine combine ritual with pragmatic things, and don't always know where one ends and the other begins. People don't believe these folks have anything to do with the Devil yet. If Christian, you probably believe saints and Christian miracles play an active part in the world. You might believe in hidden spirits. You believe there was a literal Simon Magus so in that case, you believe in &quot;magic.&quot; If you're educated you might believe that certain rituals with Christian and Platonic elements influence the world, but if you're not educated you don't know anything about that.<b> Basically, you can believe in anything if it won't really be an issue, and believing that people have heads of lions in China won't change anything in your life, so you believe mostly because you have no reason to argue. Either way, impressed scholars are more likely to believe non-sense. Supernatural explanations also make no difference whatsoever. But in the WoD, here is where things make the more difference. People are more afraid, and Dark Things Do Lurk In The Shadows. Mages, specially early on, rely on their communities to create local Patterns based on their Paradigms, so they will work hard to build in the people beliefs that reinforces their Paradigms. That makes for a lot of &quot;superstitions&quot;. Actual bygones and other monsters roaming the land make for the rest. The Mages aren't necessarily working against their communities, most even are well-meaning. Others are just monstrous bastards.</b><br /> <br /> In the World of Darkness, you might believe other things. You may know that the Order of Hermes has autonomous fortresses like monasteries, but you're supposed to stay away from them. You now that supernatural forces exist in the woods, and that some of the wise people you know might really be pagans of some kind.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I said it isn't actual Middle Ages, so that's my opinion on the above, in bold letters at the end of each sentence.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021047#post1021047\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In addition to the above:<br /> <br /> *You receive nearly six months off from work during the year because of Sundays, Saint's Day, Market Days, etc. <b>I'm not totally sure, here, but I do have to study further. Either way, I don't see the setting benefiting much from this. Darker and gritter. Probably peasants work like horses. It certainly isn't work hour all the time, and people can have a lot of idle time IF you need. And given how work is at the time, anyway, the exact time of work is more based on need than preset times. So idle time can occur at any hour without you giving people so much of it.</b><br /> <br /> *A work day is considered to be six hours because people are expected to participate in family life. If you work twelve hours straight, you are considered to have worked two days straight, and the local clergy will likely castigate any boss who regularly works their people that hard outside of planting and harvest. When combined with the previous point, the average adult person in the High Middle Ages worked around 1200 hours a year, when the extended days for planting and harvest are included. <b>Family life IS a big, big, really big thing. Have I said already that a loner is a very suspicious thing?</b><br /> <br /> *Slaves are rare outside of Eastern Europe and slave ownership of Christians is forbidden by the Church. <b>Yet they do exist. The most common mistake people do about earlier times in games is to assume that someone see slavery as inherently wrong. There will be no such a thing until the 18th century, and even them, mainly at the second half of the century. Slavery is a thing everywhere. Sometimes it is a good slice of economy and well spread. Others it is rare, since laws and practicality can make it too hard to be worth it. Also, laws do exist in regard to slaves. For most of history things weren't as bad as they got on the Americas for slaves. Of course, you can make things very bad in the WoD, but even them, most slaves that conquer liberty won't have nothing against slavery as an institution. It isn't a common thing in the medieval, though, free peasants are more productive and cost less.</b><br /> <br /> *Serfs were paid in beer and meat during planting and harvest by their lord and the Church. Serfs also had right to land and, as long as they participated in planting and harvest, would not lose their land. Outside of famine, they rarely went hungry, as hungry serfs were unproductive serfs. <b>Ownership of land is a complex thing, feudal law is very distinct from modern law. At the end of the day, the land belongs to the King, but it is to the King as institution, not as person. The children of the King, so, won't divide the land most of the time, unless they divide the crown itself. The King will lend right over land to nobles, and sometimes some princes accumulate noble titles because of that. The right over the land comes in the form of a title, so nobles with multiple titles have rights over multiple lands, that can be contiguous and in the same kingdom, or neither. Nobles can lend it further to lesser nobles, if the King lets it be so, what usually happens for major tracts of land. This goes on to the peasants, they have the right to use a tract of land they can work on, and this right can be revoked by anyone in the line. On the righer places of the hierarchy, rights are granted mainly based on the ability to defend the land, that's why military stand counts so much. At middle to low hangs, management get more of a thing, but defense is still very relevant. So it IS the duty of the lord to provide for defense. At the last place, though, peasants are only required to make the land productive.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Here I'll be more stingy, but it is only for the sake of WoD.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T19:39:25-08:00","id":1021114,"title":"","content":"Yeah, much of the sense of privacy and family life we have now were products of the little ice age. Heating small rooms with multiple fireplaces off the same chimney is considerably easier than heating a great hall off of a central fire. Later on there was a tradition of having witnesses to the consumations of marriages, but that tradition wasn't necessary when everyone slept in the same room.<br /> <br /> I'll add:<br /> <br /> *You didn't have good maps. Seafarers might have had accurate maps of port locations, but they didn't show much inland. Accurate mapping requires surveying and we didn't have many people good at that until cannons saw widespread use (surveying and aiming are the same skillset, basically). Maps were crude affairs that showed a few out of scale landmarks.<br /> <br /> *Quality of life for the poor actually went up in the last days of the (western) Roman Empire and the early Medieval period. The spread of Christianity pushed charity to new heights.<br /> <br /> *Memory was fantastic. Most people didn't know how to write things down and compensated by perfecting their memory instead. Chances are language was quite a bit more poetic too, since that serves as a useful mnemonic.<br /> <br /> *People were cleaner than we imagine. A lot of historians attribute Europe's aversion to cleanliness to diseases that spread as the Roman bathhouses broke down, or to the Black Plague (a layer of dirt does make it harder for the fleas to bite). <br /> <br /> *The Romans actually had large watermills, but like aqueducts they were a large scale endeavor. The technology wasn't lost, per se, just beyond the logistical capabilities of most communities. In that sense it was lost to those who didn't live near a surviving mill, but it was never lost completely. Concrete is the only thing I've heard of that has an actual claim on being 'forgotten,' and even that is a questionable claim.<br /> <br /> *This is the fun bit for Mage. While mystical traditions, mystery cults, astrologers, and a whole host of other magical traditions existed for millennia before this point, the Medieval Period was when our popular conception of the wizard was born. For almost a thousand years after Rome fell, if you wanted knowledge you looked to the past. Progress and thought never fully stopped, but chances were most of what the people of the day came up was already written down long ago. The image of the frail old man pulling arcane secrets out of squiggles in dusty old tomes started here.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T05:50:31-08:00","id":1021240,"title":"","content":"1)concerning local wise-medicine-women. keep in mind that what we may call &quot;didn't know where the one end ended and the other begun&quot; may not have been always true back then due to consensual reality.<br /> <br /> 2)by better teeth i assume this means that people who didn't gave them tended to die an early death?<br /> <br /> 3)i admit i have great diffuculty imagining puberty not starting at around 12(for boys) did they just stop growing between years 12-15?<br /> <br /> 4) i believe concrete included animal blood. that's why christianity was not favorable to it.<br /> <br /> 5)the romans also had a huge educated bureaucracy and true slavery.(compared to the watered down serfdom)<br /> <br /> 6)i think that this whole &quot;christian charity&quot; thing may have been a myth after all &quot;breads and circuises&quot; is pre christian right? for instance both old rome and cosntantinople received free grain from carthage and egypt respectively.<br /> <br /> 7)not all nobles were warriors back then i think. for instance if you are a count/marquiss/duke(or perhaps even baron) you're supposed to be a political judicial leader i think.?<br /> <br /> 8) why are free peasants more productive and cost less?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T06:50:27-08:00","id":1021253,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2)by better teeth i assume this means that people who didn't gave them tended to die an early death?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it means food back then didn't have sugars and hidden sugars shoveled into all their ingredients <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> People had better teeth because of that. There were way less reasons for teeth to go bad in the first place.<br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3)i admit i have great diffuculty imagining puberty not starting at around 12(for boys) did they just stop growing between years 12-15?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure about that one, either.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">6)i think that this whole &quot;christian charity&quot; thing may have been a myth after all &quot;breads and circuises&quot; is pre christian right? for instance both old rome and cosntantinople received free grain from carthage and egypt respectively.</div> </div> </div> </div>How does tending to the homeless connect to &quot;Bread and circuses&quot;?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">8) why are free peasants more productive and cost less?</div> </div> </div> </div>Because they work on their own volition, you don't need to feed them, and they in turn buy other wares.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 12-23-2016, 11:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T07:23:46-08:00","id":1021268,"title":"","content":"&quot;Because they work on their own volition, you don't need to feed them, and they in turn buy other wares.&quot;<br /> <br /> i assume you're referring to the fact that people with a relatively low income tend to spend it(thus stimulating the economy) whereas giving the local noble a bit more money will propably result in it sitting in a vault.<br /> <br /> also when we are talking about free peasants we mean serfs right? most people in the agricultural sector(that is,nearly everyone) were serfs","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T08:56:48-08:00","id":1021290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1)concerning local wise-medicine-women. keep in mind that what we may call &quot;didn't know where the one end ended and the other begun&quot; may not have been always true back then due to consensual reality.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, it could be that they not only had it right, but even had it better working because of that. In some communities. Since consensual reality is less consensual at the time, things can change drastically from a community to other depending on several factors, so the local wise-person could have it wrong anyway for following a belief from another community.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2)by better teeth i assume this means that people who didn't gave them tended to die an early death?</div> </div> </div> </div>Far less sugar, far more fibbers in the food. Also more calcium-rich aliments and more exercise in mastication. Summed up, it does wonders for teeth's health.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3)i admit i have great diffuculty imagining puberty not starting at around 12(for boys) did they just stop growing between years 12-15?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it is more for girls, as far as I know.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">5)the romans also had a huge educated bureaucracy and true slavery.(compared to the watered down serfdom)</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually the Vatican had. Rome proper watered down a lot in this period, and it is one of the reasons for people from the Renaissance saying that the Middle Ages was Dark time. The city was nearly devoid of people for some time. Today the Vatican is mostly seen as just a part of the city of Rome, but they were quite distinct things back then, and for most of History. Actually, Rome and Vatican existed as distinct power-houses in the region until the end of the 19th century (Rome recovered in the late medieval).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">6)i think that this whole &quot;christian charity&quot; thing may have been a myth after all &quot;breads and circuises&quot; is pre christian right? for instance both old rome and cosntantinople received free grain from carthage and egypt respectively.</div> </div> </div> </div>Bread and circuses was a state policy in Rome, where the Senate took some money from the tax collected and give to the miserable beggars of the city. The church, that wasn't the same as state even if having influence on it, established that a good Christian should spare some money, as the church proper also did. I'm not sure how much it actually helped, nor am I saying it was all goodness. A lot of people used it to abuse, one way or another. But it was a thing, as the poor could have a meal and some alms at the church, and rich people gave their help out of religious duty, instead of by force of state's law.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">7)not all nobles were warriors back then i think. for instance if you are a count/marquiss/duke(or perhaps even baron) you're supposed to be a political judicial leader i think.?</div> </div> </div> </div>Until the Modern State brought by the Renaissance, there was no distinction between political and military leader. The King couldn't afford to directly command such a centralized army, and land lending was the main recompense a good commander could receive for a good work, the basis of the feudal system. The local Lord, be it of any position, is indeed a political leader, a judge and an administrator. But also have to command a personal army. Their first duty to the King is to guarantee that the land is protected. If it isn't, foreigners will take it by force. If it isn't, [i]other Lords[/u] under the same King can attack it just to prove to the King that their rival is weak. They are not <u>only</u> warriors, but they have to be <u>also</u> warriors most of the time. Some can avoid this by being influential, but being a warrior yourself is always the best way to be able to command an army effectively and have their loyalty.<br /> <br /> Even today it stays true. Most active noble and royal houses keep an active interest in military activities, from hunting for sport to keep in shape to active duty in the army. Even in the palatial times of the Modern State most nobles keep their ability as warriors, and with some frequency were called upon it to keep their social stand. The nobility of Europe was built through their stance as warriors, and at the medieval it was at its apex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">8) why are free peasants more productive and cost less?</div> </div> </div> </div>First, peasants can be either serfs, villains or bourgeoisie. Second, a slave is a costly affair, since you have to take care of every need of this person. A slave needs housing, food, health care, as everyone else. It isn't a simple logistic, and without a greater system that give it the needed support, it is costly. A free person find ways to take care of themselves through several ways. And if you do it properly, this person will work for you by own volition. In the feudal system a serf working the land didn't need so much supervision, because their food comes from the same land. And if the serf don't pay tax properly, you simply take the land back. Why go through all the trouble of forced labor?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T10:04:43-08:00","id":1021311,"title":"","content":"Eh.... their teeth were pretty awful. Not due to cavities (though, depending on the diet, those too.... particularly, if they ate a lot of hazel or pine nuts), but due to wear. All that grain was ground between rocks and, effectively, they were chewing on sand when they ate their bread. Once you wear down the enamel, the breakdown of starch in the mouth into simple sugars can cause many of the same issues we face. Not nearly so much, but definitely not &quot;great teeth&quot; by modern standards. One of the differences may be due less to the presence of unrefined sugar, but due to the fact that it is delivered in a solution carbonated fluid that may be denaturing the calcium in the teeth. <br /> <br /> Also, very little functional dentistry (even &quot;pull the tooth&quot; wasn't always considered as an option). People had less problems with wisdom teeth, but seriously crooked teeth were an issue and that can cause a lot of problems. Also, if you get an abscess (which can happen without cavities, just have something slide down between the tooth and gum), particularly a maxillary abscess, you are probably going to die. <br /> <br /> Which brings up a salient point.... without penicillin, life can be much more problematic. Any infection can kill you pretty damn fast if sepsis sets in. An active life with sharp instruments leads to a lot more opportunities. There were definitely some folk treatments that worked for minor circumstances and having local, magical cunning men/wise women or priests who can perform healing miracles would help a lot, when available, but, it probably wouldn't make a significant difference for most folks. If there wasn't one in your village, chances are you wouldn't even know about them, and even if you knew about them, travel wasn't really a thing. A pilgrimage to a shrine associated with healing for a chronic condition, maybe, but if you put your scythe through someone's leg, chances were they were dead. If it was in their gut.... Take them home, put their bed so they are facing east, and have everyone come say good bye....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-23T14:21:17-08:00","id":1021399,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021240#post1021240\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> 2)by better teeth i assume this means that people who didn't gave them tended to die an early death?<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Their teeth weren't exposed to highly acidic foods, sugars or refined carbohydrates, and their diets were high in calcium as well as vitamins and minerals that supported dental health. (The plants they ate were probably more nutritious too due to less soil depletion --see <a href=\"https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/soil-depletion-and-nutrition-loss/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.scientificamerican.com/a...utrition-loss/</a>) Stone milling and simple mechanical wear were factors, as was plaque, but people also cared for their teeth. (Examples: <a href=\"http://gallowglass.org/jadwiga/herbs/teeth.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://gallowglass.org/jadwiga/herbs/teeth.html</a>)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3)i admit i have great diffuculty imagining puberty not starting at around 12(for boys) did they just stop growing between years 12-15? </div> </div> </div>The likely cause is diet. There was no interruption in growth. Most sources I've read cite 14 for the beginning of puberty (using first menstruation as a benchmark) but of course, this being a multi-year process, hormonal &quot;teenagers&quot; are going to be a couple of years older.<br /> <br /> Puberty was actually delayed more in the Renaissance due to diets being worse overall.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 7)not all nobles were warriors back then i think. for instance if you are a count/marquiss/duke(or perhaps even baron) you're supposed to be a political judicial leader i think.? </div> </div> </div>Duke comes from the Latin <i>dux, </i>for military commander. There is no separation between military and political power, and a noble household is a military installation. That said, nobles created the market for mercenaries by paying other people to take care of their military obligations. This became enough of a problem that English law made military readiness mandatory for individuals above a certain income level and empowered constables to get these people off their asses.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Writer, Game Designer, Pro since 99.<br /> <br /> Thoughts at <a href=\"http://mobunited.com\" target=\"_blank\">mobunited.com</a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/164-malcolm","name":"Malcolm","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar164_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-24T19:46:39-08:00","id":1021772,"title":"","content":"Well, the modern era experiences early puberty for a number of factors, the two most relevant are high obesity rates (body fat produces sex hormones like estrogen) and estrogen mimics (artificial chemicals found in practically everything that trick the body into thinking that it is time to enter puberty). Depending on the level of obesity and the exposure to estrogen mimics, puberty can manifest as early as eight years old (even younger puberty is generally caused by medical conditions, like the girl in Peru in the 1930s who got pregnant when she was 5 years old). <br /> <br /> Of course, even in the USA, some areas experience puberty later than others. Where I went to high school, around a quarter of the girls did not experience puberty until they were 16 and one of my friends did not enter puberty until she was eighteen (she was quite surprised when she started having sexual desires and erotic dreams)..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T00:04:27-08:00","id":1021874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021772#post1021772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where I went to high school, around a quarter of the girls did not experience puberty until they were 16 and one of my friends did not enter puberty until she was eighteen (she was quite surprised when she started having sexual desires and erotic dreams)..</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to make sure - You are essentially saying that those girls were literally flat-chested, child-hipped androgynous humans until they hit 16 and 18(!).<br /> Because that's the status quo until puberty hits. Don't confuse sexual cravings with puberty, although those usually go hand in hand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T00:51:22-08:00","id":1021891,"title":"","content":"And probably slim due to sheer hunger, maybe a not so severe case of anorexia, or another feeding disorder.<br /> <br /> But as Ambrosia said, puberty and sexual craving aren't the same. I know quite a number of women who experienced sexual cravings several years after puberty. An alarming number of them suffered from some kind of sexual abuse before puberty, but that is not a rule.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T03:20:46-08:00","id":1021919,"title":"","content":"forgive me for insisting on the last part(free labour vs slavery) but let's have this hypothetical comparison.<br /> <br /> let's say i am a very rich landowner. i have 2 hypothetical persons working for me.<br /> <br /> john and bob<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> john is a literal slave. when im not raping/beating/humiliating him he works at the field obviously he eats a small amount of what he produces or else he becomes a corpse.<br /> but other than that i give him nothing. obviously there is only so much land that can be worked. but still he gets no surplus money/product/ no vacations etc.<br /> he is a willing worker because the alternative to giving 110% is a painful death.<br /> <br /> bob is a serf. he only gives me a relatively small amount of what he produces. i can't use him for anything else..<br /> so long as he pays me his dues(like a rent) ,doesn't move from the area, and gets my permission to marry.<br /> in fact due to my feudal obligations to him i have to protect him. i can't sell him(unless i sell the land he is tied to). i can'throw him away.<br /> and in practice i believe it's relatively easy for him to get away by going in a city for a year and a day. both our ties to another are renounced.<br /> <br /> oh and concerning obesity and body shape. can i assume that the average young man back then looked like a sculpture from the hellenistic era/?<br /> after all hard work makes hard bodies right? plus there is no chocolate no sugar(unless youre filthy rich) no chemicals in foods, few &quot;weak&quot; people making it do adulthood<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;You are not that much shorter -- that happens later &quot;<br /> you mean the renaissance? why? if some maybe we ought to rethink the term?<br /> <br /> if you think about it.. religious fanaticism was at it's strongest in the 16 century. the witch hunt never actually existed before then...(local fanatics aside) renaissance men preferred art and culture to science..<br /> talk about how oil paint and statues of male nudes can inmprove an era's reputation<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T07:18:54-08:00","id":1021975,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021874#post1021874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just to make sure - You are essentially saying that those girls were literally flat-chested, child-hipped androgynous humans until they hit 16 and 18(!).<br /> Because that's the status quo until puberty hits. Don't confuse sexual cravings with puberty, although those usually go hand in hand.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, the definition of the onset of puberty in girls is first menstruation and in boys is first ejaculation. You can develop all of the secondary sexual characteristics that you want, but you do not reach puberty until you reach the milestone of either first menstruation or first ejaculation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T08:30:10-08:00","id":1021992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021975#post1021975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, the definition of the onset of puberty in girls is first menstruation and in boys is first ejaculation. You can develop all of the secondary sexual characteristics that you want, but you do not reach puberty until you reach the milestone of either first menstruation or first ejaculation.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> i thought male puberty started with the first enlargement of the penis. or am i wrong?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-25T16:33:09-08:00","id":1022130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021919#post1021919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">forgive me for insisting on the last part(free labour vs slavery) but let's have this hypothetical comparison.<br /> <br /> let's say i am a very rich landowner. i have 2 hypothetical persons working for me.<br /> <br /> john and bob<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> john is a literal slave. when im not raping/beating/humiliating him he works at the field obviously he eats a small amount of what he produces or else he becomes a corpse.<br /> but other than that i give him nothing. obviously there is only so much land that can be worked. but still he gets no surplus money/product/ no vacations etc.<br /> he is a willing worker because the alternative to giving 110% is a painful death.<br /> <br /> bob is a serf. he only gives me a relatively small amount of what he produces. i can't use him for anything else..<br /> so long as he pays me his dues(like a rent) ,doesn't move from the area, and gets my permission to marry.<br /> in fact due to my feudal obligations to him i have to protect him. i can't sell him(unless i sell the land he is tied to). i can'throw him away.<br /> and in practice i believe it's relatively easy for him to get away by going in a city for a year and a day. both our ties to another are renounced.<br /> <br /> oh and concerning obesity and body shape. can i assume that the average young man back then looked like a sculpture from the hellenistic era/?<br /> after all hard work makes hard bodies right? plus there is no chocolate no sugar(unless youre filthy rich) no chemicals in foods, few &quot;weak&quot; people making it do adulthood<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;You are not that much shorter -- that happens later &quot;<br /> you mean the renaissance? why? if some maybe we ought to rethink the term?<br /> <br /> if you think about it.. religious fanaticism was at it's strongest in the 16 century. the witch hunt never actually existed before then...(local fanatics aside) renaissance men preferred art and culture to science..<br /> talk about how oil paint and statues of male nudes can inmprove an era's reputation<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Three things happen, here. First, John won't give you 110%. He will give you enough to minimize suffering. It isn't the same. He won't have any reason to try harder than necessary to satisfy you enough. And if you seem too hard to please, it's easier for him to give up on despair, or try to escape.<br /> <br /> Second, his 100% won't be the 100% of Bob. Being treated like this, John will be overall weaker, sicker, more tired and less motivated. No, pain isn't the most effective motivation, and there's a lot of research in psychology demonstrating this. Even if you succeed in driving John to work all he can, he won't produce more than Bob simply because he turned into a less productive worker.<br /> <br /> Finally, it all resumes to cost. To keep John you have costs. Beating him costs you, watch his work costs you, guard him against fleeing costs you, keeping his necessities observed costs you. And compensating the lack of productivity will cost you more, will cost you more slaves and all that again. Oh, and you still have to protect your slaves, worse it costs even more, because they can be attacked by pillagers, but also by slave traders or by people trying to free them. Some people can try to free your slaves just to hurt your business.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Bob neither owes you little nor is likely to go away. Where Bob will go, after all? A villain <u>can</u> make a lot of money, but most won't. And it is more probable that if Bob was a serf, he won't be granted right to stay at the village as a villain. It is feasible while few people try it. You can heavily tax Bob, barely letting him with enough to live on, though it will probably not be the smartest idea to be too harsh, but lets say you are. So what? What will Bob do? Trade a safe, known life of hard work for a probable death of hunger in the wild? It isn't easy to live alone in the medieval times, without the protection of a lord. And it isn't likely to reach the land of another and plead loyalty. The next lord can be worse. The next lord can say no. If you left out of the blue the next lord will probably say no. The wild is full of predators, and every wild animal is property of the King.<br /> <br /> You can play this to a lot of drama, actually. IRL being too harsh is rarely so lucrative, but in the WoD, instead of assuming slavery as better, you can take the most of a false sense of freedom, where the serf is &quot;free&quot; to leave, but actually leaving the lord and his monstrous laws is a death sentence for most.<br /> <br /> About body shape, most people were not Greek sculpture, of course. But almost no one was fat, much less obese. People had to do a lot more exercise just for the daily chores of your own life, and food was far less fat. Saving food was a necessity, since a harsh winter could eventually be deadly, so gluttony wasn't something the others would deal with lightly. Some could afford to ignore most of this, mainly at the church, and even then, less than most people think. Fatness could occur, of course. But it wasn't likely. Actually, obesity is increasing in society fairly recently, so it isn't at all that surprising. Until the 80s not so many people were fat, that's why fat people stood so much. And for the record, I'm not saying a little chubby, I'm saying fat. A little chubby was probable fairly achievable and common, at least when food was plenty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1021975#post1021975\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, the definition of the onset of puberty in girls is first menstruation and in boys is first ejaculation. You can develop all of the secondary sexual characteristics that you want, but you do not reach puberty until you reach the milestone of either first menstruation or first ejaculation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nope. The onset of puberty <u>definitely</u> isn't the first ejaculation on boys. Simply not, seriously.<br /> <br /> Regardless of that, though, another no. By the very definition of what is puberty in human development, you can't develop any secondary sexual characteristic without puberty. It is more probable that your friends went through the first menstruation and for any reason didn't noticed or didn't talk about it. A lot of things can affect how the menstruation occurs, specially in the first years after comencing puberty. It is even possible, though extremely unlikely, that puberty comences without the first menstruation. But the very definition of puberty isn't &quot;when the first menstruation comes&quot;, but &quot;the period of hormonal transformation that causes the development of secondary sexual characteristics&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 12-26-2016, 03:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T04:34:19-08:00","id":1022273,"title":"","content":"i just remembered that a &quot;little chubby&quot; may on occasion had been seen as desirable. even pretty. after all it show that youre relatively wealthy. especially if that chubbiness is combined with a bit muscle.<br /> <br /> i admit i never gave any serious thought to the economics of slavery. if all of that is true( and it makes sense to me) then what is the practical reason that so many societies had slaves?<br /> even if they treated them better than my example(which admittedly they usually did).<br /> <br /> <br /> other than the aforementioned raping them or having them fight as gladiators.<br /> <br /> one thing tough<br /> &quot; And it is more probable that if Bob was a serf, he won't be granted right to stay at the village as a villain&quot;<br /> that's not quite so. simply because a villain/villein IS a serf he just owns more land than a bordar or cottar i think.<br /> what you are describing it free farmer or a tenant farmer. that is, someone who just pays a rent for his land. not sure if they could be throw away though.<br /> finally(although it commonly belonged to the church and not free non noblemen)there was allodial land not tied to the feudal system. essentially it belonged to someone or an institution since longer than anyone could care to remember<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T06:19:25-08:00","id":1022295,"title":"","content":"Slaves were investments. By the time of the American Civil War, one-third of the capital of the South was in slaves. People in states and nations that forbade slavery could invest in special bonds that were backed by the value of groups of slaves. <br /> <br /> The desirability of slaves come from the desire for power and social status. Slaves owners have utter power over slaves, so their slaves have no choice but to fawn over them and to serve their every depraved desire. You could also prove your wealth and status by using your slaves as visible signs of both. A man in the American South with 1,000 slaves was a man for everyone to fear because he not only had wealth and power, but he had enough of each to keep 1,000 people in slavery.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T11:11:16-08:00","id":1022399,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1492_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1492\" data-vbnamecard=\"1492\">mark</a>, I thought so about chubbiness, that's why I took the care to differentiate. Actually, chubbiness was considered desirable until very recently. Marilyn Monroe, taken as an example of beauty, was a little chubby herself (though just a little). But it should be pointed, sometimes through history being chubby wasn't easy. Either way, what was really rare was unhealthy levels of fattiness. Instead, we had unhealthy levels of slimness, but due to hungry when they happened, instead of anorexia. Or someone with metabolic tendency to be slim, a desirable trait today, but deadly back them, because you could get sick or die when the times were bad. Sometimes even the nobles and royalty had to take care with food, since overexerting the land and the subjects could have worse results at the long run.<br /> <br /> Slavery is a far more complex question than most people think, it wasn't always as it was in the Americas. Actually it was a very uncommon period on the history of slavery. Sometimes slavery was necessary because of labor that no one else would realize. At the beginning, the European colonies all over the continent had to bring a lot of workers for the farms, and free labor just wasn't enough. The symbolic aspects highlighted by <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11683_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11683\" data-vbnamecard=\"11683\">Aya Tari</a> also played a role.<br /> <br /> But I recommend a research into ancient laws about slavery, and the place of slaves in society. In the Roman Empire at the 3rd century, as an example, slaves had a lot of rights that common people didn't had in most of the modern world at the 19th century, including the right for a payment for service and to personal money and possessions, most were meant for services of high learning, like tutoring and accounting, there were clear limits to how the owner could punish the slave, and even laws for retirement of a slave. Gladiators, by the way, were almost always free men at the time, and for a slave it was a dream career, since it also wasn't anything like most people think. Battles weren't to death, and most good gladiators would retire with a decent wealth at the end of some years of work, they were essentially professional athletes (though not the most rich and popular, those are the car racers). Slavery isn't an uniform affair through history, and it changes a lot the complex logistics of it. What I said, though, is valid for the common idea of slavery, and on the context of medieval labor, where a competent work at the fields during the time of work was more important than quantity.<br /> <br /> The villain isn't an owner of land, that is what defines him. To be a villain is to be granted right to live in town, and thus so under the protection of the lord, without working the land. The villain have to find other ways to make a living. To have land and work it for a tax is what means to be a serf. Medieval communities aren't under our modern idea of freedom of movement, you do need permission to enter or live at any town. If you don't contribute in any way, the lord have very little reason to grant such permission. If your choice pisses him, he can simply revoke the permission altogether. Some lords can go as far as to exile you from the domain with a whim. In the Middle Ages there was no such a thing as a right for state protection and public spaces by principle. To be exempt of ties was pretty much to be thrown away in the wild.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T11:55:43-08:00","id":1022415,"title":"","content":"so slaves were friends with benefits<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> just joking but i got it alright.<br /> funny though that as i read more and more about history it seems that economics rathen than ideas usually played the most important role. guess karl marx was up to something<br /> anyway uhm for now at least i do have a lot of food for thought so thanks to everyone for your info!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T18:17:11-08:00","id":1022561,"title":"","content":"It's also worth noting the difference between a Slave and an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indentured_servant\" target=\"_blank\">Indentured Servant</a>. I know that how the Bible talks about slaves and the proper treatment thereof is, in fact, talking about Indentured Servants, as it speaks of debts and time periods spent working them off.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-27T09:31:12-08:00","id":1022799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1022415#post1022415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">so slaves were friends with benefits<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> just joking but i got it alright.<br /> funny though that as i read more and more about history it seems that economics rathen than ideas usually played the most important role. guess karl marx was up to something<br /> anyway uhm for now at least i do have a lot of food for thought so thanks to everyone for your info!</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really. Economics makes sense as an explanation to us because we think it's REALLY important. And that preconceived notion has been expanding rather recently. It's THE issue that creeps in whenever the &quot;Syndicate First&quot; ideas that really took hold after the Syndicate 2nd book are trotted out.<br /> <br /> BUT, that doesn't mean that our economic ideas really had any reality in the context of how the Middle Ages actually worked as a consistent system. Much of what drives modern economics just isn't present. Most of it falls apart at the granular level. This is a localized subsistence. There are times we identify issues of &quot;long distance trade&quot; that are actually anything BUT trade. They are objects showing up far away from their origins but the mechanisms that caused the displacement don't have anything to do with profitable exchange. For example, when something moves from it's point of origin to its final location due to a pilgrimage, that has nothing to do with economics. Even it's put to use in some fashion at the end. A lot of things moved around for religious reasons that may seem like trade, but that's us grafting our notions onto something by trying to make sense of what happened THEN by making an imperfect analogy to the way that a similar thing would be most likely to happen now... This is a problem interpreting the past across the board. Coins during the Roman Empire were more political markers than ways to buy things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-03T05:16:53-08:00","id":1025861,"title":"","content":"So I found out why concrete was 'lost' with the fall of the Roman empire. They couldn't actually make the ingredients. There's an important ingredient of cement called pozzolan, which is the part that reacts with water to form cementing compounds. It is named after Pozzuoli, a region near Naples where the Romans got most of the stuff. Once they withdrew to the east there was no one to ship that ingredient all around Europe, so only those people who lived near a source and knew about it could still make concrete. <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-03T05:56:33-08:00","id":1025874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1025861#post1025861\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So I found out why concrete was 'lost' with the fall of the Roman empire. They couldn't actually make the ingredients. There's an important ingredient of cement called pozzolan, which is the part that reacts with water to form cementing compounds. It is named after Pozzuoli, a region near Naples where the Romans got most of the stuff. Once they withdrew to the east there was no one to ship that ingredient all around Europe, so only those people who lived near a source and knew about it could still make concrete.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> i thought that the romans also mixed animal blood with concrete and the church saw this as to pagan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-03T06:47:21-08:00","id":1025904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>mark</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1020225-to-what-extend-did-the-dark-ages-really-suck?p=1025874#post1025874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> i thought that the romans also mixed animal blood with concrete and the church saw this as to pagan.</div> </div> </div> </div>If the church did, they were over it by the time of Charlemagne (9th century). That's the point that De Architectura was widely copied and we have a few writings from the time that indicate they knew what was in it.* I'm not finding any references to catholic objections to concrete or cement either, and as far as I can tell blood was an optional ingredient that frost proofed the concrete. It would certainly fit the feel of Dark Age Mage if the catholic church did ban concrete, but it doesn't look like they even had the opportunity to until much later.<br /> <br /> *As opposed to just copying something they didn't understand as an act of reverence, which happened <i>a lot.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 01-03-2017, 10:49 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T03:20:21-08:00","id":1060800,"title":"","content":"i was reading kindred of the ebony kingdom again and found something i had forgotten. african mundane magic. there are mundane(that is even vampires can do them) magic that protects against diseases that heals diseases,damage(better than modern medicine) and even blindness!<br /> <br /> granted it's not exactly commonplace but most people in the usa today aren't doctors either.<br /> so..if you take aids out of the equation.. then life back then(or now even in a few areas) may not have been that bad at all","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T06:55:01-08:00","id":1060854,"title":"","content":"In Wraith there are rituals to protect against Wraith that mortals can use with nothing but their willpower.<br /> Anyone can summon Demon demons with the right ritual. Mortal mystic magic it's a thing in WoD even without getting in Sorcery Paths.<br /> <br /> The thing one can't prove so easily it's that this &quot;mundane magic&quot; works because the Consensus says so. Kindred of the Ebony Kingdom doesn't say if this magic works only in traditional places of Africa, it never says that this rituals are going to stop working if you send the &quot;mage&quot; to a place that doesn't share african beliefs. (this thread was about that, wasn't it?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T07:24:45-08:00","id":1060866,"title":"","content":"well.kindred of the ebony kingdom isn't a mage book though. in mage it's quite clear that &quot;science&quot; works because the consensus says it does.<br /> also we also have a mage book :lost paths,where science fails in place like the empty quarter.<br /> <br /> by the way do you remember in which wraith book(s) i can find these rituals?-","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1492-mark","name":"mark","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1492_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1379"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-10T08:31:23-08:00","id":1060894,"title":"","content":"I think they're in the Basic, near 